      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": "Coronary subclavian steal syndrome associated with upper extremity arteriovenous fistula is an uncommon but potentially life-threatening condition. We present a case of a 65-year-old male on hemodialysis with a left upper extremity arteriovenous fistula who underwent coronary revascularization involving bypass with the left internal thoracic artery to the left anterior descending artery. Intraoperative transit-time graft flow measurements and fluorescence imaging showed a reversed flow in the left internal thoracic artery, and the left internal thoracic artery was successfully converted as a free graft from the ascending aorta to the left anterior descending artery."
      },
